The '''2022 Tameside Metropolitan Borough Council election''' took place on 5 May 2022 to elect members of [[Tameside Metropolitan Borough Council]]. This was on the same day as [[2022 United Kingdom local elections|other local elections]]. 19 of the 57 seats were up for election.<ref>{{cite web |title=Election timetable in England |url=https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/election-timetable-in-england/election-timetable-in-england#metropolitan-districts |website=GOV.UK |access-date=7 April 2022 |language=en}}</ref>

==Background==
Since its creation in 1974, Tameside has always been under [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] control, aside from 1978 to 1982 when the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservatives]] held a majority.<ref>{{cite web |title=Tameside Metropolitan Borough Council Election Results 1973-2012 |url=http://www.electionscentre.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2015/06/Tameside-1973-2012.pdf |website=Elections Centre |access-date=7 April 2022 |page=22}}</ref> In the most recent election in [[2021 Tameside Metropolitan Borough Council election|2021]], Labour lost one seat to the Conservatives, but increased their vote share by 4.6%, winning a total of 16 seats, with the Conservatives gaining 10.7% and winning 3 seats. The [[Green Party of England and Wales]] did not gain any seats, despite winning their first seat in 2019.<ref>{{cite web |title=Local Elections Archive Project - 2019 - Tameside |url=http://www.andrewteale.me.uk/leap/results/2019/39/ |website=www.andrewteale.me.uk |access-date=7 April 2022}}</ref>

The positions up for election in 2021 were last elected in [[2018 Tameside Metropolitan Borough Council election|2018]]. In that election, Labour achieved 53.8% of the vote, and won 17 seats with 1 gained from the Conservatives, while the Conservatives won 2 with 1 gain from Labour.<ref>{{cite web |title=Local Elections Archive Project - 2018 - Tameside |url=http://www.andrewteale.me.uk/leap/results/2018/39/ |website=www.andrewteale.me.uk |access-date=7 April 2022}}</ref>

